WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.500
“Add JSON” leaves the format and the previous behaviour undefined.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.667
The contract specifies a valid object with --json and unchanged text without it.

3
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:21.667
Those conditions let us reject an implementation that satisfies only half the request.

4
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:29.167
Repository instructions say how to work: do not change the public API without permission.

5
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:36.333
The spec defines this task: add --json without breaking the existing output.

6
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:43.333
Checking off a plan item changes neither obligation.

7
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:50.833
Agree on the schema first. The parser and serializer can then proceed.

8
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:58.000
The parser is ready, but the serializer is missing. The end-to-end test remains blocked.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.917
Only when both pieces are ready can the complete test run. Finishing one substep is not enough.

10
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:13.417
The plan assumes changing the serializer is enough.

11
00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:20.417
The test shows that the parser rejects --json before it reaches the serializer.

12
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:28.333
Revise the plan to address the cause. A working hypothesis is not evidence of success.

13
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:35.833
The checkpoint stores revision A, completed tests and pending decisions.

14
00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:42.833
A parser edit creates B. Dependent tests now need to run again.

15
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:50.750
On resume, reconcile reality with the checkpoint. A nearby PASS does not carry over automatically.

16
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:58.250
Valid JSON is one condition, not the whole task.

17
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:05.250
The previous output and the permitted scope must also be preserved.

18
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:12.250
Delivery requires all mandatory conditions together. One green substep is not enough.

